#### Article 38

1. Regional Government in carrying out maintenance and care must endeavor and assist children to be able to:  
a. participate;  
b. freely express opinions and think in accordance with their conscience and religion;  
c. freely receive oral or written information in accordance with the child's age stage and development;  
d. freely associate and assemble;  
e. freely rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts; and  
f. obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements.  
2. Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are developed and adjusted to the age, ability level of the child and their environment so as not to hinder and disturb the child's development.

#### Article 39

In the event a child is neglected because their parents neglect their obligations, then institutions or communities as referred to in Article 37, family or authorized officials may submit an application to the court to determine the child as a neglected child.

#### Article 40

1. Court determination as referred to in Article 39 simultaneously determines the shelter, maintenance and care for the neglected child concerned.  
2. Institutions or communities granted authority must provide a place as referred to in paragraph (1).  
3. Regional Government must conduct guidance and support the smooth care of children carried out by institutions or communities.

## Part Five

Special Protection

#### Article 41

Regional Government, communities and other state institutions are obligated and responsible to provide special protection to:  
a. children in emergency situations;  
b. children in conflict with the law;  
c. children from minority and isolated groups;  
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;  
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;  
f. children who are victims of pornography;  
g. children with human immunodeficiency virus/acquired immune deficiency syndrome;  
h. child victims of abduction, sale and/or trafficking;  
i. child victims of physical and/or psychological violence;  
j. child victims of sexual crimes;  
k. child victims of terrorist networks;  
l. children with special needs;  
m. child victims of abuse and neglect;  
n. children with deviant social behavior; and  
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.

#### Article 42

Special protection for children is carried out through efforts of:  
a. rapid response, including treatment and/or physical, psychological and social rehabilitation as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;  
b. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;  
c. provision of social assistance for children from underprivileged families; and
d. provision of protection and assistance in every judicial process.

#### Article 43

Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 41 letter a consist of:  
a. children who become refugees;  
b. child victims of unrest;  
c. child victims of natural disasters; and
d. children in armed conflict situations.

#### Article 44

Special protection for children who become refugees as in Article 43 letter a is implemented in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

#### Article 45

Special protection for child victims of unrest, disaster victims, and children in armed conflict situations as referred to in Article 43 letters b through letter d is implemented through:  
a. fulfillment of basic needs consisting of food, clothing, housing, education, health, learning, and recreation, security guarantees, and equal treatment; and  
b. fulfillment of special needs for children with disabilities and children experiencing psychosocial disorders.

#### Article 46

Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 41 letter b is implemented through:  
a. treatment of children in a humane manner taking into account needs according to their age;  
b. separation from adults;  
c. provision of legal assistance and other assistance effectively;  
d. implementation of recreational activities;  
e. freedom from torture, punishment or other treatment that is cruel, inhumane and degrading to dignity and status;  
f. avoidance of imposition of death penalty and/or life imprisonment;  
g. avoidance of arrest, detention or imprisonment except as a last resort and for the shortest period of time;  
h. provision of justice before a juvenile court that is objective, impartial and in a closed hearing;  
i. avoidance of publication of their identity;  
j. provision of accompaniment by parents or guardians and persons trusted by the child;  
k. provision of social advocacy;  
l. provision of private life;  
m. provision of accessibility especially for children with disabilities;  
n. provision of education;  
o. provision of health services; and  
p. provision of other rights in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

#### Article 47

Special protection for children from minority and isolated groups as referred to in Article 41 letter c is carried out through provision of infrastructure and facilities to be able to enjoy their own culture, acknowledge and practice their own religion, and use their own language.

#### Article 48

Special protection for children who are exploited economically and/or sexually as referred to in Article 41 letter d is carried out through:  
a. dissemination and/or socialization of provisions of Laws and Regulations related to protection of children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;  
b. monitoring, reporting, and imposition of sanctions; and  
c. involvement of various institutions or government agencies, companies, labor unions, social community institutions, and communities in the elimination of exploitation of children economically and/or sexually.

#### Article 49

Special protection for children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse as referred to in Article 41 letter e and involved in their production and distribution is carried out through efforts of supervision, prevention, treatment, and rehabilitation by Regional Government and communities.

#### Article 50

1. Special protection for children who are victims of pornography as referred to in Article 41 letter f is implemented through efforts of guidance, assistance, and social, physical health, and mental recovery.  
2. Guidance, assistance and social, physical health, and mental recovery are implemented in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.  
3. Every person must protect children from the influence of pornography and prevent children's access to information containing elements of pornography.

#### Article 51

Special protection for children with human immunodeficiency virus/acquired immune deficiency syndrome as referred to in Article 41 letter g is carried out through efforts of supervision, prevention, treatment, care, and rehabilitation.

#### Article 52

Special protection for child victims of abduction, sale, and child trafficking as referred to in Article 41 letter h is carried out through efforts of supervision, protection, prevention, care, and rehabilitation by Regional Government and communities.

#### Article 53

Special protection for child victims of physical and/or psychological violence as referred to in Article 41 letter i is carried out through efforts of:  
a. dissemination and socialization of provisions of Laws and Regulations that protect child victims of acts of violence; and  
b. monitoring, reporting, and imposition of sanctions.

