#### Article 77

1. The Regional Government plays a role in facilitating the juvenile criminal justice process based on the principle of restorative justice.
2. Facilitation of the juvenile criminal justice process as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. capacity building of human resources whose duties are related to juvenile criminal justice and the community;
b. increased coordination with law enforcement officials;
c. development of effective methods through information support, facilities and positive activities as well as taking immediate action on gaps that encourage children and/or groups of children to commit legal violations;
d. provision and delivery of legal and psychosocial assistance services in realizing the process of restorative justice and diversion for children in conflict with the law;
e. encouraging a paradigm shift in society towards children in conflict with the law;
f. developing diversion mechanisms for children in conflict with the law in accordance with Laws and Regulations;
g. developing cooperation with relevant agencies or institutions in organizing education, guidance and counseling programs for handling children who commit criminal acts, especially children under 12 (twelve) years of age to achieve optimal development; and
h. provision of supporting facilities and infrastructure for the juvenile justice process by prioritizing the best interests of the child in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
3. Further provisions regarding facilitation of juvenile criminal justice as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

## Part Eleven

Empowerment

#### Article 78

1. Empowerment is implemented in the form of:
a. facilitating the development of interests and talents for children and women;
b. developing economic enterprises for adult women;
c. developing creative spaces for children and women; and
d. assistance for children and women victims of violence.
2. Implementation of empowerment as referred to in paragraph (1) may cooperate with educational institutions, private institutions and/or non-governmental organizations.

##### CHAPTER X

##### INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

#### Article 79

1. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is the entire process of organizing women's empowerment and child protection carried out comprehensively, inclusively and integratively starting from the stage of handling reports/complaints, health services, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance up to repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of crime and violence in their social environment.
2. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System aims to:
a. improve the quality of life of women, children and family quality;
b. increase the institutional capacity of women's empowerment and child protection including the development of gender and child data; and c. provide special protection for children from various forms of violence and other discriminatory treatment.
3. The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is carried out through:
a. providing access for women and children to education, health and other strategic sectors;
b. encouraging the involvement of women and children in the development process;
c. providing knowledge, skills, character values, morals and family resilience;
d. encouraging programs that can increase women's independence in the economic, political, legal, socio-cultural and other strategic fields;
e. establishment, development and capacity strengthening of Women and Child Protection institutions including complaint service units for violence against women and children as well as legal aid services;
f. improving the quality of management human resources;
g. strengthening the institutional capacity of regional gender and child mainstreaming;
h. strengthening and developing gender and child data systems; and
i. carrying out promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts in accordance with Laws and Regulations.
4. In the framework of comprehensive, inclusive and integrative women's empowerment and child protection in the region, the Regional Government establishes:
a. P2TP2A or similar institutions that have the same functions and roles;
b. regional child protection commission or other similar institutions to support supervision of the implementation of child protection in the region; and
c. other institutions according to needs and/or provisions of Laws and Regulations.

#### Article 80

1. P2TP2A is an integrated, one-stop and networked Women's Empowerment and Child Protection technical service unit established by the government, Regional Government, community, non-governmental organizations, donor institutions or other institutions.
2. P2TP2A is positioned under the coordination of the Regional Apparatus Organization that handles women's empowerment and child protection.
3. P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) does not have the authority to formulate women's empowerment and child protection policies.
4. P2TP2A is one form of integrated service unit that functions as:
a. an information center for children and women;
b. a service center for children and women victims of violence; and
c. an empowerment center for children and women.

#### Article 81

1. P2TP2A is established based on a Mayor Decree.
2. P2TP2A membership comes from structural and non-structural elements originating from professionals, academics, community leaders and other elements as needed.
3. Further provisions regarding P2TP2A technical matters shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

#### Article 82

1. In order to increase the effectiveness of supervision of the implementation of children's rights fulfillment, the Regional Government may establish a Regional Child Protection Commission or other similar institutions to support supervision of the implementation of child protection in the region.
2. The membership of the Regional Child Protection Commission consists of 1 (one) chairperson, 1 (one) vice chairperson, and 7 (seven) members.
3. The membership of the Regional Child Protection Commission comes from elements of the Regional Government, religious leaders, community leaders, community organizations, the business world, and community groups concerned with child protection.

#### Article 83

1. The Regional Child Protection Commission has the following duties:
a. supervising the implementation of child protection and fulfillment of children's rights;
b. providing input and proposals in formulating policies on the implementation of child protection;
c. collecting data and information regarding child protection;
d. receiving and conducting reviews of public complaints regarding violations of children's rights;
e. conducting mediation on disputes regarding violations of children's rights;
f. cooperating with institutions established by the community in the field of child protection;
g. providing reports to the authorities regarding suspected violations of the Child Protection Law; and
h. other duties in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.
2. Further provisions regarding the Regional Child Protection Commission shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

#### Article 84

In the event of changes to Laws and Regulations concerning the Regional Child Protection Commission, the regulation regarding the Regional Child Protection Commission shall be guided by laws and regulations which are further regulated by Mayor Regulation.

CHAPTER XI

##### CHILD-FRIENDLY CITY

#### Article 85

To ensure the fulfillment of children's rights so that they can live, grow, develop, and participate optimally in accordance with human dignity and receive protection from violence and discrimination, real integrated efforts are needed between the Regional Government, community and business world through the development of CFC.
